---
/**
 * Chico Avenue Portfolio: investor page.
 *
 * Hidden, access-code-gated page for investors in the Chico Avenue Portfolio
 * offering (Bidwell Chico Avenue Portfolio, LLC). Not linked in site nav, noindex.
 * URL: /chico-avenue
 *
 * Three jobs: let an investor pull the underwriting levers and see what happens
 * to their money, read the business plan, and get to the offering documents in
 * the investor portal.
 *
 * Engine: /chico-avenue/model.js (a port of the two per-building pro formas).
 * Access code: compared as a SHA-256 hash (ACCESS_HASH in the inline script).
 * To change the code: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update('newcode').digest('hex'))"
 */
const PORTAL_URL = 'https://bidwellcapital.invportal.com/';
const OFFERING_URL = 'https://bidwellcapital.invportal.com/app/offering-detail/6a838d2dbe73b41cde69e270';
const DECK_URL = '/chico-avenue/Chico-Avenue-Portfolio-Investor-Deck.pdf';
---

<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow, noarchive" />
  <title>Chico Avenue Portfolio | Bidwell Capital</title>
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var stored = null;
      try { stored = localStorage.getItem('theme'); } catch (e) {}
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', stored || 'light');
    })();
  </script>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ============================================================ AUTH -->
<div id="auth-screen">
  <div class="auth-wrap">
    <img src="/logo-black.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="auth-logo logo-light" />
    <img src="/logo-white.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="auth-logo logo-dark" />
    <div class="auth-card">
      <span class="section-label">Confidential</span>
      <h1 class="auth-title">Chico Avenue Portfolio</h1>
      <p class="auth-sub">Enter the access code from your invitation.</p>
      <input type="password" id="auth-input" placeholder="Access code" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Access code" />
      <div id="auth-error" class="auth-error" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <button id="auth-btn" class="btn btn-primary">Continue</button>
    </div>
    <p class="auth-foot">For invited investors only. If you were not sent this link by Bidwell Capital, please do not proceed. This page is not an offer to sell securities.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ APP -->
<div id="app" hidden>

  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="wrap topbar-inner">
      <a href="/" class="brand">
        <img src="/logo-black.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="brand-logo logo-light" />
        <img src="/logo-white.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="brand-logo logo-dark" />
      </a>
      <nav class="topnav" aria-label="Sections">
        <a href="#model">Model</a>
        <a href="#plan">Business plan</a>
        <a href="#properties">Properties</a>
        <a href="#terms">Terms</a>
        <a href="#documents">Documents</a>
      </nav>
      <a href={PORTAL_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-outline btn-sm">Investor Portal ↗</a>
    </div>
    <nav class="subnav" aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#model">Model</a><a href="#plan">Plan</a><a href="#properties">Properties</a><a href="#terms">Terms</a><a href="#documents">Documents</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <img src="/chico-avenue/1017-esplanade.jpg" alt="1017 Esplanade, Chico" class="hero-img" />
    <div class="hero-shade"></div>
    <div class="wrap hero-inner">
      <span class="section-label">Confidential · September 2026 · Supplements the PPM, which controls</span>
      <h1>Chico Avenue Portfolio</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">Two apartment buildings on the Esplanade in downtown Chico, 36 units total. We have them under contract from an estate at about $111,000 a door after seller credits. Same crews and same approach as 946 Cherry, ten blocks away.</p>
      <div class="hero-stats">
        <div><b>36</b><span>units, two buildings</span></div>
        <div><b>~$111K</b><span>per door, net of credits</span></div>
        <div><b id="hero-irr">12.6%</b><span>projected 10-year annual return (IRR), base case, pre-tax*</span></div>
        <div><b id="hero-mult">2.3x</b><span>projected equity multiple, base case*</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">*Projections, not guarantees. Assumptions and risks are below. The Private Placement Memorandum and Supplement No. 1 control.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a href="#model" class="btn btn-primary">Run the numbers</a>
        <a href="#plan" class="btn btn-outline">Read the plan</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ MODEL -->
  <section id="model" class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">The model</span>
        <h2>The numbers behind the offering</h2>
        <p>These start at our underwriting. Change anything and the returns update on whatever amount you put in. At our assumptions this matches the per-building sheets we sent the lender. If you move away from them, the model gets stricter than the sheets: a refinance has to pass a 1.25x coverage test, the loans reset or mature on their own schedule, and if a building runs short the shortfall shows up as a capital call.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="model-grid">
        <!-- LEVERS -->
        <aside class="levers" id="levers">
          <div class="lever-group">
            <div class="lever-row-head">
              <span class="lever-title">Scenario</span>
              <button type="button" id="btn-reset" class="link-btn">Reset</button>
            </div>
            <div class="seg" id="presets">
              <button type="button" data-preset="down"><span class="lbl-long">Everything goes wrong</span><span class="lbl-short">Bad case</span></button>
              <button type="button" data-preset="base" class="active">Underwriting</button>
              <button type="button" data-preset="up">Upside</button>
              <button type="button" data-preset="custom" class="seg-custom" hidden>Custom</button>
            </div>
            <p class="lever-note">"Everything goes wrong" stacks ten bad assumptions at once. Once you move a slider it is your scenario, not our projection.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="lever-group">
            <label class="lever-title" for="inp-invest">Your investment</label>
            <div class="money-input"><span>$</span><input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="inp-invest" value="100,000" /></div>
            <p class="lever-note" id="invest-note">$100,000 minimum; the Manager may accept less.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="lever-group">
            <span class="lever-title">Main levers</span>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rentAdj">Rents vs. our target</span><output id="out-rentAdj" for="inp-rentAdj">0%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-rentAdj" min="-15" max="10" step="0.5" value="0" aria-labelledby="lbl-rentAdj" />
              <div class="lever-hint" id="hint-rentAdj"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-capRate">Cap rate at sale</span><output id="out-capRate" for="inp-capRate">6.50%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-capRate" min="5.5" max="8.5" step="0.1" value="6.5" aria-labelledby="lbl-capRate" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Lower cap rate means a higher building value. Also used to size the refinances.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-hold">Sell after</span><output id="out-hold" for="inp-hold">10 years</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-hold" min="3" max="10" step="1" value="10" aria-labelledby="lbl-hold" />
              <div class="lever-hint">The plan is a 5 to 10 year hold at the Manager's discretion.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <details class="more" id="more-levers">
            <summary>More levers</summary>

            <div class="lever-group">
              <span class="lever-title">Portfolio</span>
              <div class="seg" id="selection">
                <button type="button" data-sel="both" class="active">Both</button>
                <button type="button" data-sel="p1017">1017 only</button>
                <button type="button" data-sel="ra">Royal Arms only</button>
              </div>
              <p class="lever-note">Each escrow stands alone. If a property does not close, the money allocated to it is refunded.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="lever-group">
              <span class="lever-title">Operations</span>
              <div class="lever">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-stab">Years to reach target rents</span><output id="out-stab" for="inp-stab">2 years</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-stab" min="2" max="5" step="1" value="2" aria-labelledby="lbl-stab" />
                <div class="lever-hint">Underwriting reaches our targets in year 3. A slower lease-up stretches that.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="lever">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rentGrowth">Rent growth per year, after that</span><output id="out-rentGrowth" for="inp-rentGrowth">3.0%</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-rentGrowth" min="0" max="5" step="0.25" value="3" aria-labelledby="lbl-rentGrowth" />
              </div>
              <div class="lever">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-vacancy">Vacancy</span><output id="out-vacancy" for="inp-vacancy">4.0%</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-vacancy" min="2" max="10" step="0.5" value="4" aria-labelledby="lbl-vacancy" />
                <div class="lever-hint">Plus 3% every year for move-in specials and unpaid rent.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="lever">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-expGrowth">Expense growth per year</span><output id="out-expGrowth" for="inp-expGrowth">2.5%</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-expGrowth" min="1" max="5" step="0.25" value="2.5" aria-labelledby="lbl-expGrowth" />
                <div class="lever-hint">Property taxes grow at the 2% Prop 13 cap regardless.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="lever">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rehab">Renovation budget vs. plan</span><output id="out-rehab" for="inp-rehab">On plan</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-rehab" min="0" max="50" step="5" value="0" aria-labelledby="lbl-rehab" />
                <div class="lever-hint">Plan is about $18,750 per unit at 1017 and $15,500 at Royal Arms. An overrun raises the total raise.</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lever-group">
              <span class="lever-title">Debt</span>
              <div class="lever">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rateDelta">Purchase loan rates</span><output id="out-rateDelta" for="inp-rateDelta"></output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-rateDelta" min="-1" max="2" step="0.125" value="0" aria-labelledby="lbl-rateDelta" />
                <div class="lever-hint">1017: Five Star Bank expression of interest, about 6.6%, 12 months interest-only, five-year fixed then reset. Royal Arms: 6.5% modeled, five years interest-only; no term sheet yet.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="lever lever-toggle">
                <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="inp-refiOn" checked /><span class="toggle-track"></span><span>Include the refinances</span></label>
                <div class="lever-hint">Royal Arms refinances at the start of year 4 and 1017 at year 5, sized at the loan-to-value below and capped at 1.25x coverage. Off means each loan runs to its own reset or maturity after year 5.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="lever lever-toggle" id="lever-refi2">
                <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="inp-refi2On" checked /><span class="toggle-track"></span><span>Include the second refinances in year 9</span></label>
                <div class="lever-hint">Returns most of the remaining capital before sale but costs fees, so it trims the IRR slightly.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="lever" id="lever-refiRate">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-refiRate">Refinance rate</span><output id="out-refiRate" for="inp-refiRate">6.50%</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-refiRate" min="5" max="9" step="0.125" value="6.5" aria-labelledby="lbl-refiRate" />
              </div>
              <div class="lever" id="lever-refiLTV">
                <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-refiLTV">Refinance loan size, % of value</span><output id="out-refiLTV" for="inp-refiLTV">65%</output></div>
                <input type="range" id="inp-refiLTV" min="50" max="70" step="5" value="65" aria-labelledby="lbl-refiLTV" />
                <div class="lever-hint">A bigger loan returns more capital sooner and leaves less at sale.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </details>
        </aside>

        <!-- RESULTS -->
        <div class="results">
          <div class="mini-kpis" id="mini-kpis" aria-live="polite"></div>
          <div class="kpis" id="kpis"></div>
          <div class="events" id="events" hidden></div>

          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-head">
              <h3>Cash back to you, running total</h3>
              <div class="legend">
                <span><i style="background:var(--c-cash)"></i>Cash flow</span>
                <span><i style="background:var(--c-refi)"></i>Refinance proceeds</span>
                <span><i style="background:var(--c-sale)"></i>Sale proceeds</span>
                <span id="legend-call" hidden><i style="background:var(--c-call)"></i>Capital call</span>
                <span><i class="legend-line"></i>Your investment</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chart-dist" role="img" aria-label="Annual distributions on your investment"></canvas></div>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-head"><h3>Year by year</h3></div>
            <p class="table-note" id="table-note"></p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table class="pf" id="table-years"></table>
            </div>
            <p class="fine">Cash yield is your cash flow for the year divided by your original investment. NOI is net operating income (rent less operating costs) for the whole portfolio; coverage is NOI divided by loan payments, and lenders typically require at least 1.25x.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="two-col">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Sources and uses</h3>
              <table class="su" id="table-su"></table>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
              <h3>What we get paid</h3>
              <p class="card-lede">All of this is already taken out of the returns above. Property management is paid to us (Bidwell Capital Fund GP, LLC), not to a third party.</p>
              <table class="su" id="table-gp"></table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="fine">Projections, unaudited, based on the assumptions shown. Not a guarantee. Scenarios you build are yours, not ours. Our fees and 20% share are deducted; not included are bridge-loan interest if subscriptions come in after closing, offering costs, any loan prepayment penalty, and your taxes. Distributions are modeled once a year; actual distributions are quarterly. The Private Placement Memorandum, as supplemented, controls. See the disclaimer at the bottom of the page.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ PLAN -->
  <section id="plan" class="block block-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">Business plan</span>
        <h2>What we plan to do</h2>
        <p id="plan-lede">Combined, the two properties are underwritten at about $283K of year-1 net operating income (NOI, rent less operating costs). The plan takes that to about $383K by year 3 without betting on the market. Here is how.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><b>1</b><h3>Buy them right</h3><p>Both buildings come from an estate, each in its own escrow, and we set a walk-away price on each before we started. We negotiated $229,000 of repair credits off the inspection reports, most of it for the Royal Arms roof.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><b>2</b><h3>Cut the expenses</h3><p>We manage the buildings ourselves, so there is no third-party management markup. The on-site manager at Royal Arms goes away when we take over. Insurance, landscaping and pest control get re-bid, and we bill utilities back to residents on all 36 units where the leases and California law allow it.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><b>3</b><h3>Turn the units</h3><p>As units turn, our crew renovates them for about $15,500 to $18,750 each: LVP floors, counters, plumbing and electrical fixtures, new cabinet doors. Then we lease at market. We do a handful first to see what rent they actually get before we spend the whole budget.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><b>4</b><h3>Refinance</h3><p>The model refinances Royal Arms in year 4 and 1017 in year 5 at 65% of the new value, which gets about half of your money back while we keep both buildings. It refinances again in year 9 and returns most of the rest. The other option at that point is a 1031 exchange into a bigger building, which defers the tax.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="plan-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="section-label">Why downtown Chico</span>
          <p class="plain">Nobody has built small apartment buildings downtown in years, and nothing is proposed near these blocks. At about $111,000 a door we are well under what it costs to build in Chico now. These buildings have been held a long time, the units are mostly original, and the rents are below market. Rents catch up when units turn.</p>
          <p class="plain">Once stabilized, the buildings should earn about 8.2% on what we paid, renovated and spent in fees. Stabilized buildings around here have been selling at 6.0% to 6.5% cap rates. <span id="exit-cap-note"></span></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="section-label">Where the value comes from</span>
          <p class="plain">Rents today average $1,171 at 1017 and $1,053 at Royal Arms. We underwrite $1,404 and $1,329 by year 3, about 20% and 26% higher. Most of that comes from leases turning over, not from the renovations.</p>
          <p class="plain">Most residents already pay a $50 utility fee. We bring every unit onto the same billing and keep the $35 resident-benefit fee that now goes to a screening vendor, where the leases and California law allow. Insurance was re-quoted off the seller's surplus-lines policy, services get re-bid, and the model already carries the higher property taxes after reassessment. The rents we model are below what I think fully renovated units will get.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-wide">
          <span class="section-label">946 Cherry Street, year one (our building ten blocks away, not part of this offering)</span>
          <p>We bought 946 Cherry in August 2025 for $1.46M. 20 studios, similar tenants, same crew. We barely renovated anything. Rents went up 15.7% because we billed utilities back from day one, did maintenance and turns with our own W-2 crew, and reset each lease to market when it turned.</p>
          <div class="proof">
            <div><b>99.2%</b><span>trailing-12 occupancy</span></div>
            <div><b>+34%</b><span>NOI vs. what we underwrote ($130,165 vs. $96,867)</span></div>
            <div><b>+15.7%</b><span>average rent since acquisition</span></div>
            <div><b>$360</b><span>total delinquency across 20 units</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="fine">Unaudited, from the property's books through July 2026, compared with our own acquisition pro forma (available on request). 946 Cherry is owned by an affiliate of the Manager, is studios rather than one and two bedrooms, and is not part of this offering. How one building did does not predict how this one will.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-wide">
          <span class="section-label">Timeline</span>
          <div class="timeline">
            <div><b>Oct 2026 (target)</b><span>Close both escrows, subject to financing and the offering minimum. Management rollover ready for day one.</span></div>
            <div><b>2026</b><span>Reset. Billing, taxes, insurance and services re-bid before takeover.</span></div>
            <div><b>2027 to 2028</b><span>Reposition. Interior turns in phases. A test group proves the ceiling first.</span></div>
            <div><b>2029 to 2030</b><span>Stabilize. Full rent roll at market. Season the income for the refinance.</span></div>
            <div><b>2030+</b><span>Refinance or move up. Cash flow on a reduced basis, or 1031 into a larger building.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ PROPERTIES -->
  <section id="properties" class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">The properties</span>
        <h2>The two buildings</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="props" id="props"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ TERMS -->
  <section id="terms" class="block block-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">Terms</span>
        <h2>Terms</h2>
        <p>Short version. The operating agreement, PPM, Supplement No. 1 and subscription agreement control.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="terms">
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Split</span><p>You get 80% of the cash flow, we get 20%. There is no preferred return, so we share from the first dollar.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Capital first</span><p>When we refinance or sell, you get all of your capital back before anything is split 80/20.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Fees</span><p>2% acquisition fee on the contract price, $84,200 at closing. 5.5% of collections for property management, paid to us. 1% of revenues for asset management. 1% refinance and guaranty fee on each refinance. No fee when we sell. All of it is already out of the returns shown; the dollars are in "What we get paid" above.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Distributions</span><p>Quarterly, once the buildings are producing. Year one is about 3.7% on your money while units turn, so do not count on much income the first year.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Offering</span><p>Up to $4,000,000 of Class A Units under Rule 506(b), same terms for everyone regardless of when you come in. $100,000 minimum, though we may accept less. Money sits at Chase and comes back without interest if we do not reach $600,000. Each subscription is split 32.46% to 1017 and 67.54% to Royal Arms, and if one building does not close, that piece is refunded rather than moved to the other. We expect to take about $2.23M to $2.5M. We plan to hold 5 to 10 years.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Debt</span><p>1017: $975,000 from Five Star Bank per their expression of interest, which is not yet a commitment. 5-year Treasury plus 2.25%, about 6.6% today, fixed for five years then reset, 10-year term, 12 months interest-only then 25-year amortization. I personally guarantee it, there is a 1.25x coverage covenant, and a prepayment penalty in years 1 to 5. Royal Arms: about $1.7M modeled at 6.5% with five years interest-only. No bank has issued a term sheet on Royal Arms yet.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Structure</span><p>Bidwell Chico Avenue Portfolio, LLC owns 1017 directly and owns Royal Arms through a subsidiary so each building can have its own lender. Bidwell Capital Fund GP, LLC is the manager. We manage the buildings ourselves and do not hold a California broker license; the PPM risk factors cover this.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Sponsor alignment</span><p>We plan to put in at least $10,000 on the same terms as everyone else. The amount is not fixed and may come out of the acquisition fee. I personally guarantee the 1017 loan and get the 1% guaranty fee above for it. We run the buildings ourselves. The PPM lists our fees and conflicts.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="risks">
        <span class="section-label">Risks</span>
        <ul class="bullets">
          <li>The return comes from turning units and running the buildings well. If leasing is slow or the renovations run over, everything moves right. The "years to reach target rents" and renovation sliders show what that does.</li>
          <li>Neither loan is committed yet. Worse terms, or no loan on Royal Arms, means lower returns or no Royal Arms. Both loans are interest-only for a stretch and then reset or come due in year 5, and the plan counts on being able to refinance.</li>
          <li>Royal Arms is about two thirds of the money. One building, one roof, one plan. Your interest in it sits behind its lender through a subsidiary.</li>
          <li>Year 1 is today's rents. We assume rents step up in year 2 and hit our targets in year 3 as units turn, about 20% higher at 1017 and 26% at Royal Arms. Chico rents have come down from their 2024 peak. If that keeps going, those steps take longer or do not happen.</li>
          <li>Property taxes about double when the buildings are reassessed at sale, and that is in the model. City sewer rates jump in late 2026. We plan to bill that back where the leases and California law allow.</li>
          <li>The Royal Arms roof certification is from the seller's roofer, depends on some minor repairs, and pushes replacement out rather than eliminating it. Renovations can cost more than budgeted.</li>
          <li>If subscriptions come in after closing we may borrow short term from Wes Hill at about 1.1% a month. That is not in the model.</li>
          <li>You cannot sell these units, there is no redemption, and there is no public market. Plan on five years or more.</li>
        </ul>
